Well, Dave, since you asked...

Generally, I think Nethack is one of the best games I've ever played; possibly
the best, period.  (I find it somewhat ironic that I've spent a fair amount of
money on games, and yet I spend most of the time in which I play games playing
Nethack, which is, of course, free).  Obviously, at least to the experienced
addict, there are many things in the game which may be abused.  Here are a few
suggestions for limiting a couple.  (IMHO, most of these abuses should be 
allowed enough to make the game easier, but not enough to make it a cakewalk.
of course, getting to the point where you can use most abusive strategies 
isn't a cakewalk, but...)

1.  The cursed scroll of genocide;  once you've cursed genocided a creature,
	trying to genocide it again should result in a message something like
	"but that monster's already been genocided!"  That way, you can only
	gate in wraiths once.  I went up probably at least 13 levels from
	genociding wraiths...

2.	If you have 10 scrolls of polymorph wombat (p in your inventory), and
	you #dip p into a potion of holy water, you should end up with p - 
	9 scrolls of polymorph wombat and q - a blessed scroll of polymorph
	wombat.  This would slightly lessen holy water abuse.  It might be
	reasonable to make an exception in the case of #dipping potions of
	water, otherwise you might not be able to make additional holy water...

3.	On the ring of conflict, the end-game, and the castle level:  I really
	don't think it's *that* overpowerful when you get that far.  By the 
	time I get to the castle level, I can get through it without too much
	in the way of problems (especially since I usually genocide Lichs;
	the game I won was an exception...)  Where the ring of conflict comes
	in handy is:  a)  pit fiends.  having them bash eachother rather than
	me is a *good thing*.  b)  when the wiz gates in 20 monsters, it's
	nice to see them bashing on him rather than on me....

	On the other hand, the "checkerboard" idea has a lot of appeal...

4.	The end-game.  Soup it up.  By the time I got to the end-game, there
	just wasn't anything there to challenge me.  The only monster which
	did any significant damage to me was the wiz himself... by the time
	you get there, dragons are no problem.  (The lichs were rather a pain,
	since I couldn't get the ring of conflict off, and they just kept 
	bopping eachother with cold...)  Maybe some nasty monsters which only
	show up on the end level...  or give 'em wands and let them use 'em...  :)



Well, that's about all I can think of at the moment.  Maybe more ideas later...
